cker). The community cards are unconnected and unsuited, offering no flush or straight possibilities. The opponent has checked on the pre-flop, flop, turn, and river, indicating a weak hand, potentially a small pair or Ace-high. While your hand is weak, betting now after checking two streets looks suspicious and may induce a call from a weak pair or Ace-high, costing you more chips. Since the pot is small (likely 200 chips), the risk of a bluff outweighs the potential reward. Checking is the prudent play to see the showdown for free, minimizing loss if beaten.\n\nReason: You have no made hand (High Card K, Q kicker). The opponent has shown extreme weakness, but bluffing into a small pot after checking twice is risky and often lacks credibility. Checking minimizes variance and loss.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
